<p>The successful replay recorded <strong>{{ replay.decisionUsage.calls }} decision calls</strong> and <strong>{{ replay.decisionUsage.totalTokens }} decision tokens</strong>, separately from {{ replay.llmUsage.totalTokens }} chat-model tokens. These are one run's observations, not a price or performance benchmark.</p>

<h2 id="what-the-replay-proves">What the replay proves</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Turns</th><th>Observed contract</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1–6</td><td>The router selects criterion owners; invalid calendar and budget values remain with their owner.</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>A date correction made while amenities are active reaches <code>DateRangeStep</code>, then returns to amenities in the same turn.</td></tr>
<tr><td>9–11</td><td>The normalized record can be reviewed, then passes the decision gate before search and grounded presentation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>12–15</td><td>A budget revision produces an empty result, remains recoverable, then produces a new reviewed result set.</td></tr>
<tr><td>16</td><td>The selected hotel is resolved against saved results, an exact confirmation is returned, and the session completes.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<p>One successful replay does not establish general routing or judge accuracy. The deterministic contract in lesson 7 covers branches and outages; a labeled evaluation set is still required to calibrate production thresholds.</p>

